    <h2>What makes Level 52 tricky<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The first three moves are three different gestures on three different fixtures, and not one of them is a drag. The speaker is clicked, the window is double-tapped, the laptop is swiped downward. That opening teaches nothing repeatable: the rhythm you find at the speaker fails at the window, where the tap has to be doubled. Drags do not begin until step 4, so a quarter of the level goes on learning three separate input styles.</p>
    <p>From step 4 the level turns into a series of hand-offs to things rather than people. Headphones go to the monster, the battery to the monkey toy, the shoes to the trap, the saw blade to the antlers, the bullet to the rifle, the dried fish to the tabby cat on the wall. Six destinations and none of them is the man you are getting out, several of them tiny: a toy on the floor, a trap, a cat on the wall. He is never a target in this level, which is the opposite of what a rescue looks like.</p>
    <p>The last three moves stay inside two objects. The radio is clicked, then its volume knob is swiped right — a part of the thing you just touched, the only time the level asks you to operate a sub-part. Then the finish leaves the furniture altogether: swipe up the fireplace exhaust pipe. It is the only upward gesture in the level, the pipe is scenery, and it arrives after eleven moves that all ended on an object or a creature. That is where a run stalls with everything else already handled. No arrow points at the pipe either; it is drawn like every other fixture in the room.</p>

    <h2>Click, double tap, swipe, then drag<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Four input types in twelve moves, and the failures cluster at the changes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Speaker: one click. Window: a double tap. Laptop: a downward swipe (steps 1–3).</li>
      <li>The window is the only double tap in the level; nothing else wants the gesture twice.</li>
      <li>The six drags in steps 4–9 end on the monster, the monkey toy, the trap, the antlers, the rifle and the cat on the wall — never on the man.</li>
      <li>The battery belongs to the monkey toy, not to the radio or the laptop.</li>
      <li>Click the radio before the volume knob will move, then swipe the knob right (steps 10–11).</li>
      <li>The last move is an upward swipe on the fireplace exhaust pipe, which is scenery rather than a person.</li>
      <li>Twelve moves; the pipe comes last, and it is the easiest one to leave.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Every move in this room is about noise, and the pipe is the way out rather than another thing to quiet.</p>

    <h2>FAQ<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
   
    <details><summary>What is the answer to Brain Puzzle 3 Level 52?</summary><p>In order: 1) Click the speaker on the wall; 2) Double-tap the window; 3) Swipe down on the laptop; 4) Drag the headphones to the monster; 5) Drag the battery to the monkey toy; 6) Drag the shoes to the trap; 7) Drag the saw blade to the antlers; 8) Drag the bullet to the rifle; 9) Drag the dried fish to the tabby cat on the wall; 10) Click the radio; 11) Swipe right on the volume knob; 12) Swipe up the fireplace exhaust pipe</p></details>
